bantu's book tv  our visit to chico california continues  with local author robert cottrell as he  explores the counter culture of the  1960s and some of the influential people  and movements that helped define it the  name of my book is sex drugs and rock  and roll the rise of america's 1960s  counter culture  my book focuses on  kind of the whole of american bohemia to  a certain extent so i go back and talk  about early progenitors i talk about  figures like  edgar allan poe and walt whitman  i talk about  members of the lyrical left in the early  20th century john reed louise bryant  i talk about the hipsters  and then i focus on the beats i get two  figures of the cast of alan ginsberg and  jack kerouac who are two of the central  biographical elements in this book  and then i go on and talk about timothy  leary kankeezy  and then a whole  array of  characters who course through the decade  of the 1960s  when we think of the counter culture of  the 1960s when we think of the hippies  we think of literally millions  of participants in one capacity or  another  and they were  individuals who were  disenchanted with  matters as they were in some fashion  they were concerned about the greatest  stain on the american body republic  jim crow its continuance  they were deeply concerned about the war  in vietnam  so those are kind of two of the twin  pillars in a matter of speaking  but they were also concerned about  elemental  issues they were concerned about  family relationships  and personal dealings and  sexuality  and consciousness and rationality  and  there was this coalescing of  forces  some demographics some economic  some literary some cultural some  political in nature that  melded together and provided this kind  of backdrop  that allowed for the  counter culture of the 1960s to be as  large in scope and scale as it proved to  be there were several sparks or triggers  for the counter culture of the 1960s i  think you have the backdrop of the cold  war  internationally speaking and  domestically  unfolding simultaneously  i think you had that  feeling and that  reality of alienation that i referred to  earlier  i think the the demographic matters came  into play too you had these large pools  of young people congregating together  often on or near college university  campuses  and then you had those terrible  realities the terrible realities of  racism and war  and i think that  enabled a large number of young people  to be receptive  to  different possibilities  and then you had these  key figures  like ginsburg and kerouac  and leary and keezy but you also had  the the great bands of the era  you know  uh and the great musicians  of a more solitary cast you know from  bob dylan to the beatles to the san  francisco sound groups airplane  quicksilver grateful dead and so forth  and i think that  young people were  were drawn  to messages that were being imparted  messages of a  challenging of a probe being of a  provocative  caste  and sometimes of a of a radical nature  contesting the verities that were in  place  contesting american institutions and  even contesting  the way people  uh interacted with one another the way  people fought and the way they behaved  there's a very radical facet of the  counterculture that i that i highlight  um  there were revolutionary possibilities i  think inherent in some of this  which is why it's perceived to be as  dangerous as as it was considered by  some of the powers that were and it was  treated accordingly  um  there are other  avenues  there's the setting up of an underground  press syndicate so underground  newspapers cropped up  there's  new kind of  comics that are in in place during this  period we think of robert crum and  and  gilbert shelton and others  the rag is the underground newspaper in  in austin uh in the 1960s and i talked  about it again in that chapter on on  that  texas  capitol city  and  it was an this incredible  compendium  that publication was i mean it you can  go back and look at the issues and you  can get these snapshot looks  of so much that was going on vis-a-vis  the counterculture but the other  movements of the of the entire period  really interesting matter to reflect on  and i grapple with this as best i can in  the book  is the question of how universal in  scope and nature  the counterculture proved to be because  we think of the counter culture we think  a granite village in new york city we  think of haight ashbury in san francisco  we think of other pockets venice you  know above la and so forth  but the reality is  where young people could congregate  you could find  seeds for and then the planting of the  counterculture  which means virtually  any  college university  had the possibilities  uh inherent  in  the  fact that there was a community  of like-minded people that would be  present  so for instance one of the focal points  in one of the chapters is on austin  texas the university of texas  and people would kind of  question  why that would be included but  austin and the university of texas were  epicenters  in their own fashion for  the new left for the anti-war movement  for the counter-culture for the women's  movement  so i used that as kind of a microcosmic  examining process just as i focus on  hate not surprisingly when you get into  1967.  earlier in scope  you can talk about the  appearance at columbia university of the  small circle of friends it was referred  to in that manner it was ginsburg and  kerouac  and  their buddies  who were  challenging  the institution that was colombia and  they were challenging the literary  establishment  and they were  questioning malik as ginsburg referred  to it  as he does in his famous epic poem howl  as carorack does it on the road so  the delivery of howl which was presented  in public for the first time in 1955  the publication of on the road two years  later those are a couple of those  seminal moments  and they serve as triggers  the  appearance the  emergence of the psilocybin project at  harvard believe it or not  which involved timothy leary and then  eventually richard alpert  and  brought in  young people to serve as kind of guinea  pigs as it were  and proved to be enormously  controversial  surprisingly what became most  controversial for the august institution  that was harvard was the fact that  undergraduates were included in the mix  it seemed to be okay if it were  community members  professors or graduate students but  where harvard drew the line was with  undergraduates the mary pranksters  their journey across america in the  summer of 1964.  they went to meet kerouac in new york  city it was not a happy experience  kerouac had shifted very far to the  political right by that point in time  they went to meet  leary and alpert at millbrook estate  above new york city and that was not a  happy experience either  leary in fact didn't even greet the  pranksters even though they had gone 3  000 miles for that encounter to take  place  you get later into the the 1960s the mid  60s and the latter  portion of it all together  and it's almost like there's one event  after another  there are the anti-war protests  in  berkeley and oakland and in the bay area  in general  that involved  ginsburg and the pranksters and the  hell's angels  there are the acid test that the  pranksters put on 66  67  there's the human being  be in  in san francisco golden gate park in  january of 1967  there's the summer of love in 1967.  there's  there's woodstock there's altamonte  there's uh  the unveiling of the the family the  killings by  charles manson's  gang  in late 1969.  so  again it's it's almost as if there were  these um ethical points  that eventuated  and some  spoke to the better side  or the angelic angelic qualities  contained within the counterculture  some spoke to the darker side  um and you end up with people's own  frailties and their own weaknesses and  the  despoilation that drug usage brings  about and  all the sexually transmitted diseases  that are ushered in and  and then the lost lives  that  are such a part of the story and  you know it's we think of  the successes but  there are those who burned out along the  way i mean they're they're the famous  musicians  right who who died because of drug  overuse  correct  so you think of  in you know almost in one fell swoop  when  um  janice and jimmy and jim  died  janice joplin and jimi hendrix and jim  morrison in a matter of months 70 and 71  but there are  countless  lesser-known figures  who  their  you know education became stunted and  their occupational possibilities became  blunted  stillborn  and some of the people never recovered  they never recovered  you know their minds didn't recover and  their psyches didn't recover and their  damaged relationships were not  re-knitted  um  so there's  you know lots of serious aspects to the  to the counter culture and  some as i said before  you know seemingly were of this  millennial possib possibility wise are  some of them seemingly  reach to the stars right  in a millennial-like manner but  in some ways it was the apocalyptic  realities that  helped to  dampen  the hopes  of  how transformative this might have been  talked about this at the the near close  of the book  the computer  revolution  that we've experienced over the last few  decades the pc revolution silicon valley  at all a lot of that is believe it or  not connected with the counter culture  and  part of it is  pertaining to  who was drawn to those kinds of pursuits  and  the  the  attempt  to reach a different kind of  consciousness in different ways  and  just as the u.s  intelligence agencies and the u.s  military  helped to spawn the counterculture  believe it or not  through  drug tests  that people like  ken kesey partook off  and then helped to spread the gospel  about  um  the military is heavily involved in  birthing the computer revolution of the  the nineteen  and beyond so there's this  weird kind of symbiotic relationship  between the counter culture and  the us military and intelligence  operatives  and i'm not saying that this is a  conspiratorial in nature i'm not  suggesting that  i'm what i'm stating is the fact that  there is this  twining of these  threads  and um  that's one of the reason why the  counterculture's impact has been as  enduring as it's been  it's often been viewed in a very  character  caricatured manner  there have been cliches drawn about it  there have been stereotypes that have  been cast around it  it's largely been dismissed and  denigrated  and i think it needs to be  received and perceived for the serious  cultural  and potentially political force that it  was  um  my argument is that this was a radical  unfolding  with revolutionary  possibilities  uh did the revolution play out was  certainly not the in the manner that had  been hoped for by those who participated  in the counter culture i think that's  obviously the case  but  again  the impact nevertheless of the counter  culture  was was huge  and remains so today  book tv is on the campus of california  state university at chico this  university is the second oldest  university

